Title: The Pilings

**Hamburg — March 2nd, 1:12 AM**

The pier smelled of wet rope and diesel smoke, harbor lights bleeding up through fog six berths down. Tomas stood at the bollard with his collar up, water tracing the scar through his eyebrow. *She came alone. She actually came alone.*

"You brought the weather," he said, not turning.

"I brought the debt." Behind him, Raina's boots struck once on the wet stone and stopped — close enough to push, far enough to choose. "Five years. You remember the number?"

He turned slow. She was smaller than he remembered — or maybe he'd just gotten used to being afraid of her. Rain slicked her hair flat against her skull, made her look like something carved from a figurehead.

"I remember the number," he said. "I also remember the reason."

"Reasons don't pay debts."

"No," he said. "But they buy time."

Her hand moved — not to a weapon, just to her coat. He didn't flinch. She noticed. Something in her expression shifted, just a degree. Respect, maybe. Or disappointment.

"You've got thirty seconds," she said. "Then I stop being reasonable."

Tomas stepped away from the edge and walked toward her, hands visible, steady. When he was close enough to see the water beading on her eyelashes, he stopped.

"I don't need thirty," he said. "I need five."

He pulled the envelope from his jacket and held it out. She didn't take it immediately — just looked at him, then at it, then back.

"All of it?"

"All of it."

She took the envelope. Opened it with one hand, thumb flicking through the bills without looking down. Her eyes stayed on his the whole time.

"This buys you clear," she said. "Not friends."

"I'm not asking for friends."

She slid the envelope inside her coat and stepped back. The rain was falling harder now, drumming on the containers stacked behind them. A foghorn sounded out in the roads, low and long. For a second neither of them moved.

Then Raina turned and walked toward the gangway. She stopped at the edge, one boot on the rail.

"Tomas."

"Yeah."

"Don't make me come back."

She dropped through the hatch and was gone. Tomas stood there another minute, watching the rain pool on the stone, then pulled his collar tighter and headed for the lights.

